From 7cbdd5567198c01acd8c1e2536ae39bd108a4dbd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Farhan Riaz <its-forum@techlogix.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 20:50:59 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] core, render, desktop: Stop unloadAndStop collection
 storms; make resident assets cheap

Live testing of AdventureQuest Worlds after the corrected library lifetime
fix reported two things after ten map switches: the process at 3.5 GB, and
"lagging hard".

The lag was ours. `Loader.unloadAndStop(true)` charged the collector a
whole collection's worth of debt on every call, gc-arena carries artificial
debt across cycles and pays all of it in one call, and a game disposes one
loader per asset when it tears a zone down - dozens of calls in a frame,
each buying a full mark-and-sweep of a heap that grows with the session.
The call now sets a request on the update context; `Player::update` grants
it with one full cycle at the end of the update, at most once every ten
frames, and leaves requests inside that window to the paced collector,
which the libraries' external-memory accounting already keeps in step.
Forty loaders disposed in one frame on a 100k-object heap: 4.4 s of stalls
over the following sixty frames before, 2.0 s (the frame budget) after.

The memory was mostly what a resident asset costs, multiplied by the several
hundred assets the game keeps resolvable in its ApplicationDomain on purpose:

* Every DefineShape was tessellated into a GPU mesh at load, and kept, with
  up to four scaled variants, for as long as its library lived. Shapes are
  now tessellated on first draw, and `Player::update` drops the meshes of
  shapes not drawn for twenty seconds.
* Every shape kept its parsed records (`swf::Shape`), tens of kilobytes for
  a few hundred bytes of SWF. `Graphic` keeps the tag's slice instead and
  reads the records back on demand; they are dropped with the meshes.
* `BitmapData` pixel buffers and `cacheAsBitmap`/filter textures were
  invisible to the collector's pacing: a room's worth of discarded avatar
  bitmaps sat unreachable until a cycle happened to run. Both now report
  their bytes to `Metrics` as external allocations.

The memory report gains the render backend's counters (wgpu `counters`
feature), Ruffle's own counts of live meshes and textures with their bytes,
and the bytes live in the Rust allocator (a counting global allocator in the
desktop player), so a run can be attributed instead of guessed at.

In a logged-in session with ten zone changes between three busy rooms the
process went from 1.9 GB (Rust heap 790 MB, most of it parsed shapes and
lingering bitmaps) to about 1.0 GB (Rust heap 480 MB, of which the
ActionScript heap is 160 MB), with no growth from the fifth switch on and
no change in what is rendered.
